<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonian front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Salonika front · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Macedonian front, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonika</a> front, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Allied forces landed at Salonika in October 1915 and, joined by the rebuilt Serbian army, held a line against Bulgarian and German troops for nearly three years. Their breakthrough at Dobro Pole in September 1918 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> to sign an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> and opened the way to the liberation of Serbia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonian-front.jpg" alt="Macedonian front" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salonika_Army_Christmas_Card_1916_displaying_soldiers_of_all_Allied_nations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>'s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in August 1914 failed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s attitude became decisive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Bulgaria had fought Serbia in 1885 and in the Second Balkan War of 1913, and felt that Serbia had seized land that was rightfully Bulgarian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> could offer only small concessions, whereas the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> promised most of what Bulgaria claimed. After the Allied defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> and the Russian defeat at Gorlice-Tarnów, King Ferdinand signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 21 September 1915 Bulgaria began to mobilise. Two Bulgarian armies attacked on 15 October, cutting the Serbian army off from the Greek border, and only about 125,000 Serbian soldiers reached the Adriatic coast after a retreat through Montenegro and Albania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first Allied troops, two divisions sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> under the French commander Maurice Sarrail, landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonika</a> on 5 October 1915, too late to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Pushed back by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, they were all back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> by 12 December and entrenched around Thessaloniki, earning the mocking name Gardeners of Salonika. The Serbian army, rested on Corfu, was brought to the front by the French. Greece was officially neutral, but King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> was pro-German and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> pro-Allied. After the Bulgarian offensive of August 1916 took Greek territory east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Struma</a>, Venizelist officers staged a coup, and Greece had two governments, a division known as the National Schism.</p><p>By spring 1917 the Allied Army of the Orient had grown to 24 divisions, but its April offensive failed. In June Constantine was exiled and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. The Greek army, a third of the Allied force, took part in the Battle of Skra-di-Legen on 30 May 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>General Franchet d'Espèrey won agreement for a grand offensive in September 1918. After the bombardment for the Battle of Dobro Pole began on 14 September, the French and Serbians attacked and captured their objective the next day, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and the British were stopped at Doiran on 18 September. Some Bulgarian units then surrendered positions without a fight and the Bulgarian command ordered a retreat.</p><p>Mutinous Bulgarian troops gathered at Radomir, and on 27 September leaders of the Bulgarian Agrarian National Union proclaimed a republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was granted the Armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> on 29 September, in force from noon on 30 September, and Ferdinand I abdicated on 3 October. Winston Churchill, in The World Crisis, saw the defeat of Bulgaria as the start of events that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in November 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macedonian front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greece entered the First World War on the Entente side in 1917 and fought on the <b>Macedonian front</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The 135,000 soldiers of the Serbian Army retreated through Albania to Corfu and in 1916 joined the <b>Salonika front</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Royal Yugoslav Army was built on the Serbian army that had retreated through Albania in 1915 and returned in triumph after the breakthrough on the <b>Salonika front</b> in September 1918, and its officer corps remained overwhelmingly Serbian, which alienated Croats and Slovenes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First World War French forces of the <b>Salonika front</b> occupied Korçë in late 1916.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-albanian-republic-of-korce">Autonomous Albanian Republic of Korçë</a></li></ul></section>
